1
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:40,500
CONVERSATION 1
Tonight, we have a very special guest.

2
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:48,360
Mrs. Anna Sanchez is a three-time Olympic champion and author of the new book To the Edge.

3
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:51,340
Mrs. Sanchez, thank you for joining us.

4
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,000
Thank you for having me.

5
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,840
Let's start with your book.

6
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:57,600
What does the title "To the Edge" mean?

7
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,340
What are you referring to?

8
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:05,780
The book is about how science and technology has helped push humans to the edge of their physical abilities.

9
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:11,920
I argue that in the past 20 years, we have had the best athletes the world has ever seen.

10
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:14,240
But is this a fair comparison?

11
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:19,740
How do you know how, say, a football player from 50 years ago would compare to one today?

12
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:22,140
Well, you are right.

13
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,680
That comparison would be perhaps impossible to make.

14
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:32,540
But the point is more about our knowledge today of human biochemistry, nutrition, and mechanics.

15
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:39,960
I believe that while our bodies have not changed in thousands of years, what has changed is the scientific knowledge.

16
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:44,240
This has allowed athletes to push the limits of what was previously thought possible.

17
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:46,740
That's interesting.

18
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:50,340
Please tell us more about these perceived limits.

19
00:01:50,340 --> 00:02:03,800
The world is seeing sports records being broken that could only be broken with the aid of technology, whether this be the speed of a tennis serve or the fastest time in a hundred-meter dash or 200-meter swimming race.

20
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:09,820
Is there any concern that technology is giving some athletes an unfair advantage over others?

21
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:11,860
That is an interesting question.

22
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:15,820
And one that has to be considered very carefully.

23
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:23,760
Skis, for example, went from being made of wood to a metal alloy which allows for better control and faster speed.

24
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:26,480
There is no stopping technological progress.

25
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:40,600
But, as I said, each situation should be considered carefully on a case by case basis.

26
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:57,700
Q1. What do we learn about Anna Sanchez?

27
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:14,880
Q2. What is the woman's book mainly about?

28
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:33,080
Q3. What has changed in the past thousands of years?

29
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:53,260
Q4. What is the man's concern about the use of technology in sports competitions?

30
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:56,880
CONVERSATION 2
I've worked in international trade all my life.

31
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,500
My father did so too before me.

32
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,360
So I guess you could say it runs in the family.

33
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,660
What products have you worked with?

34
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:05,580
All sorts, really.

35
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:13,940
I've imported textiles, machinery, toys, solar panels, all kinds of things over the years.

36
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:16,020
Trends and demand come and go.

37
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:20,120
So one needs to be very flexible to succeed in this industry.

38
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:21,180
I see.

39
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,360
What goods are you trading now?

40
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:29,660
I now import furniture from China into Italy and foods from Italy into China.

41
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:31,600
I even use the same container.

42
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:34,840
It's a very efficient way of conducting trade.

43
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:36,380
The same container?

44
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:39,920
You mean you own a 40-foot cargo container?

45
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:41,160
Yeah, that's right.

46
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:45,460
I have a warehouse in Genoa, Italy and another in Shanghai.

47
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:50,360
I source mid-century modern furniture from different factories in China.

48
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:52,640
It's very good value for money.

49
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:58,580
I collect it all in my warehouse and then dispatch it to my other warehouse in Italy.

50
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:07,160
Over there I do the same, but with Italian foods instead of furniture, things like pasta, cheese, wine, chocolates.

51
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:13,120
And I send all that to my warehouse in China in the same freight container I use for the furniture.

52
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:19,200
So I presume you sell both lines of products wholesale in each respective country.

53
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:20,140
Of course.

54
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:23,940
I possess a network of clients and partners in both countries.

55
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:26,640
That's the main benefit of having done this for so long.

56
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:30,180
I've made great business contacts over time.

57
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:32,380
How many times do you ship?

58
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:39,000
I did 12 shipments last year, 18 this year, and I hope to grow to around 25 next year.

59
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,820
That's both ways, there and back again.

60
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:46,120
Demand for authentic Italian food in China is growing rapidly.

61
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:52,500
And similarly, sales of affordable, yet stylish wooden furniture are also increasing in Italy.

62
00:05:52,500 --> 00:06:06,720
Furniture is marginally more profitable, mostly because it enjoys lower customs duties.

63
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:26,800
Q5. What does the woman think is required to be successful in international trade?

64
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:45,460
Q6. What does the woman say is special about her way of doing trade?

65
00:06:45,460 --> 00:07:03,560
Q7. What does the woman have in both Italy and China?

66
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:52,060
Q8. What does the woman say makes furniture marginally more profitable?

67
00:07:52,060 --> 00:08:06,620
PASSAGE 1
Too many people view their jobs as a five-day prison, which they are paroled every Friday, says Joel Goodman, founder of the Humor Project, a humor consulting group in Saratoga Springs, New York.

68
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:20,600
Humor unlocks the office prison because it lets adults bring some of their childlike spirit to the job, according to Howard Pollio, professor of psychology at the University of Tennessee Knoxville.

69
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:27,880
An office with humor breaks is an office with satisfied and productive employees.

70
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:35,400
Pollio conducted a study that proved humor can help workers excel at routine production tasks.

71
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:38,220
Employees perform better when they have fun.

72
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:46,620
In large corporations with a hierarchy of power, there is often no outlet for stress.

73
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:59,100
Every company needs underground ways of poking fun at the organization, says Lynn Mark, a speaker on workplace humor for Saint Mary's Health Center in St. Louis.

74
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:07,320
Kodak's Rochester, New York branch, discovered a way for its 20,000 employees to uncork their bottled-up resentments.

75
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:13,600
Their 1,000 square foot humor room features a toy store.

76
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:22,940
Among the room's many stress-reducing gadgets, the main attraction is a boss doll with detachable arms and legs.

77
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:30,480
Employees can take the doll apart as long as they put its arms and legs back in place.

78
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:42,840
Sandy Cohan, owner of a graphic print production business, created the quote board to document the bizarre phrases people say when under strict deadlines.

79
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:47,360
"When you're under stress, you say stupid things," says Cohen.

80
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:59,680
"Now we just look at each other and say, that's one for the quote board."

81
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:18,080
Q9. What does the passage say about humor in the workplace?

82
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:35,820
Q10. What does the study by Howard Pollio show?

83
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:54,540
Q11. What can Kodak's employees do in the humor room?

84
00:10:54,540 --> 00:11:00,740
PASSAGE 2
Public interest was aroused by the latest discovery of a changed gene in obese mice.

85
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:05,680
The news was made known by Rockefeller University geneticist Jeffrey Friedman.

86
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:15,380
The researchers believe this gene influences development of a hormone that tells an organism how fat or full it is.

87
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:26,260
Those with the changed gene may not sense when they have eaten enough or if they have sufficient fatty tissue, and thus can't tell when to stop eating.

88
00:11:26,260 --> 00:11:32,840
The researchers also reported finding a gene nearly identical to the mouse obesity gene in humans.

89
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:38,820
The operation of this gene in humans has not yet been demonstrated, however.

90
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:46,080
Still, professionals like University of Vermont psychologist Eyster Ross Bloom reacted enthusiastically.

91
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:56,200
This research indicates that people really are born with a tendency to have a certain weight, just as they are to have a particular skin color or height.

92
00:11:56,200 --> 00:12:08,120
Actually, behavioral geneticists believe that less than half of the total weight variation is programmed in the genes, while height is almost entirely genetically determined.

93
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:12,200
Whatever role genes play, Americans are getting fatter.

94
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:19,600
A survey by the Center for Disease Control found that obesity has increased greatly over the last 10 years.

95
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:27,340
Such rapid change underlines the role of environmental factors like the abundance of rich foods in Americans' overeating.

96
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:37,440
The Center for Disease Control has also found that teens are far less physically active than they were even a decade ago.

97
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:41,320
Excepting that weight is predetermined can relieve guilt for overweight people.

98
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:55,180
But people's belief that they cannot control their weight can itself contribute to obesity.

99
00:12:55,180 --> 00:13:14,160
Q12. What does the speaker say has aroused public interest?

100
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:32,540
Q13. What do we learn about the changed gene?

101
00:13:32,540 --> 00:13:52,460
Q14. What does University of Vermont psychologist Eyster Ross Bloom say?

102
00:13:52,460 --> 00:14:40,700
Q15. What accounts for Americans' obesity according to a survey by the Center for Disease Control?

103
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:50,200
RECORDING 1
Qualities of a relationship such as openness, compassion and mental stimulation are of concern to most of us regardless of sex.

104
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:57,100
But judging from the questionnaire response, they are more important to women than to men.

105
00:14:57,100 --> 00:15:04,600
Asked to consider the ingredients of close friendship, women rated these qualities above all others.

106
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:20,040
Men assigned a lower priority to them in favor of similarity in interests, selected by 77% of men, and responsiveness in a crisis, chosen by 61% of male respondents.

107
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:28,520
Mental stimulation ranked 3rd in popularity by men as well as women, the only area of overlap.

108
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:34,260
Among men, only 28% named openness as an important quality.

109
00:15:34,260 --> 00:15:37,960
Caring was picked by just 23%.

110
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:44,760
It is evident by their selections that when women speak of close friendships, they are referring to emotional factors.

111
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:49,520
While men emphasized the pleasure they find in a friend's company.

112
00:15:49,520 --> 00:16:01,500
That is, when a man speaks of a friend, he is likely to be talking about someone he does things with—a teammate, a fellow hobbyist, a drinking buddy.

113
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:04,580
These activities are the fabric of the friendship.

114
00:16:04,580 --> 00:16:11,340
It is a "doing" relationship in which similarity in interests is the key bond.

115
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:15,300
This factor was a consideration of less than 11% of women.

116
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:23,160
Women opt for a warm, emotional atmosphere where communication flows freely.

117
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:25,960
Activity is mere background.

118
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:31,920
Lastly, men, as we have seen, have serious questions about each other's loyalty.

119
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:40,780
Perhaps this is why they placed such strong emphasis on responsiveness in a crisis—someone I can call on for help.

120
00:16:40,780 --> 00:16:50,200
Women, as their testimonies indicate, are generally more secure with each other and consequently are more likely to treat this issue lightly.

121
00:16:50,200 --> 00:17:01,100
In follow-up interviews, this was confirmed numerous times as woman after woman indicated that being there when needed was taken for granted.

122
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:09,080
As for the hazards of friendship, more than a few relationships have been shattered because of cutthroat competition and feelings of betrayal.

123
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:13,860
This applies to both men and women, but unequally.

124
00:17:13,860 --> 00:17:19,860
In comparison, nearly twice as many men complained about these issues as women.

125
00:17:19,860 --> 00:17:34,300
Further, while competition and betrayal are the main thorns to female friendship, men are plagued in almost equal amounts by two additional issues: lack of frankness and a fear of appearing unmanly.

126
00:17:34,300 --> 00:17:46,920
Obviously, for a man, a good friendship is hard to find.

127
00:17:46,920 --> 00:18:07,980
Q16. What quality do men value most concerning friendship according to a questionnaire response?

128
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:26,240
Q17. What do women refer to when speaking of close friendships?

129
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:45,960
Q18. What may threaten a friendship for both men and women?

130
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:58,300
RECORDING 2
The partial skeletons of more than 20 dinosaurs and scattered bones of about 300 more have been discovered in Utah and Colorado at what is now the Dinosaur National Monument.

131
00:18:58,300 --> 00:19:08,460
Many of the best specimens may be seen today at museums of natural history in the larger cities of the United States and Canada.

132
00:19:08,460 --> 00:19:15,400
This dinosaur pit is the largest and best-preserved deposit of dinosaurs known today.

133
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:29,660
Many people get the idea from the mass of bones in the pit wall that some disaster, such as a volcanic explosion or a sudden flood, killed a whole herd of dinosaurs in this area.

134
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:34,100
This could have happened, but it probably did not.

135
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:40,400
The main reasons for thinking otherwise are the scattered bones and the thickness of the deposit.

136
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:52,260
In other deposits where the animals were thought to have died together, the skeletons were usually complete and often all the bones were in their proper places.

137
00:19:52,260 --> 00:19:55,600
Rounded pieces of fossil bone have been found here.

138
00:19:55,600 --> 00:20:01,520
These fragments got their smooth, round shape by rolling along the stream bottom.

139
00:20:01,520 --> 00:20:07,900
In a mass killing, the bones would have been left on the stream or lake bottom together at the same level.

140
00:20:07,900 --> 00:20:16,000
But in this deposit, the bones occur throughout a zone of sandstone about 12 feet thick.

141
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:26,060
The mixture of swamp dwellers and dry land types also seems to indicate that the deposit is a mixture from different places.

142
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:32,040
The pit area is a large dinosaur graveyard—not a place where they died.

143
00:20:32,040 --> 00:20:40,720
Most of the remains probably floated down an eastward flowing river until they were left on a shallow sandbar.

144
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:46,220
Some of them may have come from faraway dry land areas to the west.

145
00:20:46,220 --> 00:20:52,840
Perhaps they drowned trying to cross a small stream, or were washed away during floods.

146
00:20:52,840 --> 00:20:58,860
Some of the swamp dwellers may have got stuck in the very sandbar that became their grave.

147
00:20:58,860 --> 00:21:04,120
Others may have floated for miles before being stranded.

148
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:14,860
Even today, similar events take place: When floods come in the spring, sheep, cattle, and deer are often trapped by rising waters and often drown.

149
00:21:14,860 --> 00:21:26,200
Their dead bodies float downstream until the flood recedes, and leaves them stranded on a bar or shore where they lie, half buried in the sand until they decay.

150
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:46,060
Early travelers on the Missouri River reported that shores and bars were often lined with the decaying bodies of buffalo that had died during spring floods.

151
00:21:46,060 --> 00:22:05,620
Q19. Where can many of the best dinosaur specimens be found in North America?

152
00:22:05,620 --> 00:22:25,400
Q20. What occurs to many people when they see the massive bones in the pit wall?

153
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:45,000
Q21. What does the speaker suggest about the large number of dinosaur bones found in the pit?

154
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:53,140
RECORDING 3
I would like particularly to talk about the need to develop a new style of aging in our own society.

155
00:22:53,140 --> 00:23:00,420
Young people in this country have been accused of not caring for their parents the way they would have in the old country.

156
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:01,920
And this is true.

157
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:08,780
But it is also true that old people have been influenced by an American ideal of independence and autonomy.

158
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:15,920
So we live alone, perhaps on the verge of starvation, in time without friends.

159
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:18,580
But we are independent.

160
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:23,660
This standard American style has been forced on every ethnic group.

161
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:33,680
Although there are many groups for whom the ideal is not practical, it is a poor ideal and pursuing it does a great deal of harm.

162
00:23:33,680 --> 00:23:39,820
This ideal of independence also contains a tremendous amount of unselfishness.

163
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:46,560
In talking to today's young mothers, I have asked them what kind of grandmothers they think they are going to be.

164
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:55,240
I hear devoted loving mothers say that when they are through raising their children, they have no intention of becoming grandmothers.

165
00:23:55,240 --> 00:24:07,180
They are astonished to hear that in most of the world, throughout most of its history, families have been three or four generation-families living under the same roof.

166
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:13,240
We have over-emphasized the small family unit—father, mother, small children.

167
00:24:13,240 --> 00:24:19,580
We think it is wonderful if grandma and grandpa, if they're still alive, can live alone.

168
00:24:19,580 --> 00:24:26,040
We have reached the point where we think the only thing we can do for our children is to stay out of their way.

169
00:24:26,040 --> 00:24:31,680
The only thing we can do for our daughter-in-law is to see as little of her as possible.

170
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:43,480
Old people's nursing homes, even the best ones, are filled with older people who believe the only thing they can do for their children is to look cheerful when they come to visit.

171
00:24:43,480 --> 00:24:50,340
So in the end, older people have to devote all their energies to not being a burden.

172
00:24:50,340 --> 00:24:57,560
We are beginning to see what a tremendous price we've paid for our emphasis on independence and autonomy.

173
00:24:57,560 --> 00:25:03,080
We've isolated old people and we've cut off the children from their grandparents.

174
00:25:03,080 --> 00:25:10,880
One of the reasons we have as bad a generation gap today as we do is that grandparents have stepped out.

175
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:30,920
Young people are being deprived of the thing they need most: perspective—to know why their parents behave so peculiarly and why their grandparents say the things they do.

176
00:25:30,920 --> 00:25:48,700
Q22. What have young Americans been accused of?

177
00:25:48,700 --> 00:26:08,420
Q23. What does the speaker say about old people in the United States?

178
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:27,740
Q24. What is astonishing to the young mothers interviewed by the speaker?

179
00:26:27,740 --> 00:26:51,121
Q25. What does the speaker say older people try their best to do?
